---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Best Peloton Alternative (2026): 6 Bikes and the App Math";
const description =
  "Six bikes that replace a Peloton, checked against makers and reviews: Schwinn IC4, BowFlex C6, NordicTrack X24, Keiser, Wahoo. Peloton App+ vs All-Access costs.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "schwinn-ic4",
    name: "Schwinn IC4",
    brand: "Schwinn",
    category: "Indoor cycling bike, works with the Peloton App",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Schwinn+IC4+Indoor+Cycling+Bike&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$799–$999",
    priceBounds: {"low":799,"high":999},
    specs: "100 magnetic resistance levels · 40 lb flywheel · 330 lb max user · 54.6 x 30.7 in. footprint · dual-sided SPD and toe-cage pedals · 10-yr frame, 3-yr parts and electronics, 1-yr labor warranty",
    pros: [
      "The cheapest good way to ride Peloton classes: about half the three-year cost of a Peloton with All-Access",
      "Schwinn lists Peloton, Zwift, JRNY, Kinomap and Explore the World, so you are not locked to one app",
      "Quiet magnetic resistance and a 330 lb rating",
      "Dual-sided SPD and toe-cage pedals take ordinary trainers or SPD cleats",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No screen: you supply a tablet or phone",
      "The Peloton App cannot read or set its resistance, so you follow cues by feel and the console",
      "Schwinn's pages give two different rider height ranges",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For most people who want Peloton classes without Peloton prices. Pair it with App+, not App One.",
    description: "The IC4 is an indoor cycling bike without a built-in screen, and for a Peloton alternative that is the point: you bring a tablet, run the Peloton App, and pay $24 a month for App+ rather than $44 for All-Access. Schwinn's page and independent reviews from Garage Gym Reviews and OutdoorGearLab agree on 100 levels of magnetic resistance, a 40 lb flywheel, a 330 lb maximum user weight, a 54.6 by 30.7-inch footprint and dual-sided pedals with SPD clips and toe cages. The warranty is 10 years on the frame, three years on parts and electronics and one year of labor. Garage Gym Reviews names it its best Peloton alternative for studio classes. It connects over Bluetooth, which Schwinn describes as partial FTMS, and lists JRNY, Peloton, Zwift, Kinomap and Explore the World, each with its own subscription. On 19 October Schwinn had it at $899 against a $999 list price, and an Amazon listing for Schwinn's IC series showed $799.99 that day, so check which model the price applies to. Schwinn's specification table gives a rider height of 5 ft 1 in. to 6 ft 4 in. but its FAQ says 4 ft 6 in. to 6 ft 6 in.; confirm before ordering if you are near either end.",
  },
  {
    id: "bowflex-c6",
    name: "BowFlex C6 Bike",
    brand: "BowFlex",
    category: "Indoor cycling bike, the IC4 under the BowFlex name",
    badge: "Same Bike, Other Badge",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=BowFlex+C6+Bike&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$899–$999",
    priceBounds: {"low":899,"high":999},
    specs: "100 magnetic resistance levels · 40 lb flywheel · 330 lb max user · 54.6 x 30.7 in. footprint · dual-sided SPD and toe-cage pedals · 10-yr frame, 3-yr parts and electronics, 1-yr labor warranty",
    pros: [
      "Mechanically the same bike as the IC4, so the same Peloton App setup works",
      "BowFlex lists Peloton, Zwift and JRNY compatibility over Bluetooth",
      "Takes any standard 9/16-inch adult bicycle pedal, per BowFlex",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No screen and no automatic resistance, as with the IC4",
      "Pays to compare: the C6 and IC4 go on and off sale at different times",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Buy whichever of the C6 and IC4 is cheaper on the day; they are the same machine.",
    description: "BowFlex and Schwinn Fitness are sister brands now run by Johnson Health Tech, and the C6 is the IC4 under the BowFlex name. BowFlex's page gives the same 54.6 by 30.7-inch footprint, 330 lb maximum user weight, belt-driven 40 lb flywheel with 100 magnetic resistance levels, dual-sided SPD and toe-cage pedals and 10-year frame, three-year parts and electronics, one-year labor warranty as the IC4; OutdoorGearLab treats the two as one design, and Garage Gym Reviews lists the C6 with the same figures. BowFlex lists JRNY, Peloton and Zwift compatibility, says the pedals take any standard 9/16-inch adult bicycle pedal, and gives a recommended rider height of 5 ft 1 in. to 6 ft 4 in. It was $899 against a $999 list price on our 19 October check for our exercise bike guide. It is here because the two bikes go in and out of stock and on and off sale at different times, and knowing they are the same machine stops you paying more for a name.",
  },
  {
    id: "nordictrack-x24",
    name: "NordicTrack X24 Bike",
    brand: "NordicTrack",
    category: "Incline/decline screen bike with iFIT",
    badge: "Best Bike+ Alternative",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0DK7QZQBM?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/311CG4fIleL._SL500_.jpg","width":426,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$1,999–$2,799.99",
    priceBounds: {"low":1999,"high":2799.99},
    specs: "24 resistance levels · -10% to 20% incline and decline · 24 in. pivoting HD touchscreen · 350 lb max user · 10-yr frame, 2-yr parts warranty",
    pros: [
      "The closest thing to a Peloton Bike+ from another brand: big pivoting screen and automatic resistance",
      "Tilts from -10% decline to 20% incline, which no Peloton bike does, and iFIT can change it for you",
      "A 350 lb rating, the joint highest here",
      "Can be ridden in manual mode with a free iFIT account, NordicTrack says",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Paid iFIT Pro membership, $39 a month on 19 October, is required with purchase for content and features",
      "Larger and heavier than a studio bike; Garage Gym Reviews scores it low for portability",
      "A closed platform: the screen is built around iFIT",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For someone who wants a screen bike that changes its own resistance and incline, and is happy to pay iFIT rather than Peloton.",
    description: "The X24 is NordicTrack's current flagship bike and Garage Gym Reviews' pick for best overall Peloton alternative. NordicTrack and Garage Gym Reviews agree on 24 resistance levels, an incline and decline range of -10% to 20%, a 24-inch pivoting HD touchscreen and a 350 lb user capacity. With an iFIT membership, NordicTrack says the bike adjusts resistance and incline automatically to follow the trainer; without one, you control both from the handlebars in manual mode with a free iFIT account. NordicTrack's site also says a paid iFIT Pro membership is required with equipment purchase to access content and features, and iFIT listed iFIT Pro at $39 a month on 19 October. NordicTrack's included warranty is 10 years on the frame, two years on parts and one year of labor; Garage Gym Reviews gives the same frame and parts terms. On 19 October NordicTrack listed it at $1,999.99 against a $2,799.99 list price, and Amazon listed it at $1,999. Garage Gym Reviews gives its dimensions, but we could not confirm them on NordicTrack's page, so they are not on the card.",
  },
  {
    id: "keiser-m3i",
    name: "Keiser M3i Indoor Bike",
    brand: "Keiser",
    category: "Studio-grade indoor bike, no platform of its own",
    badge: "Best Premium",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00LSX8AA0?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31b09AaUZOL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $2,985",
    priceBounds: {"low":2985,"high":2985},
    specs: "Magnetic resistance, 24 gears · fits riders 4 ft 10 in. to 7 ft · 350 lb max user · rear flywheel, Poly-V belt drive · Bluetooth M Connect display",
    pros: [
      "The widest fit range here, agreed by Keiser, Amazon and independent retailers",
      "A studio-grade frame and a 350 lb rating, built for heavy daily use",
      "No platform of its own: use the Peloton App, Zwift or nothing at all",
      "Rear flywheel keeps the drivetrain away from sweat, according to Keiser",
    ],
    cons: [
      "More than three times the price of an IC4",
      "A small display and no screen; reviewers criticise both",
      "Machine weight differs between Keiser and the Amazon listing, so we have printed neither",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For households with very different riders, or anyone who wants a bike that will outlast several app subscriptions.",
    description: "The M3i is a long-standing studio bike, and as a Peloton alternative its case is fit and durability rather than features. Keiser says its V-shaped frame fits riders from 4 ft 10 in. to 7 ft with a 350 lb capacity, and the Amazon listing, independent reviews and retailers agree. Resistance is magnetic, with 24 gears on the shifter, through a Poly-V belt, and the flywheel sits at the rear. The M Connect display sends data to apps over Bluetooth, so the Peloton App, Zwift or any other app works on your own tablet, each with its own subscription. Reviewers praise the ride and criticise the small display. The bundle with media tray and display was $2,985 on Amazon on our 16 October check and on 19 October, and an independent review site gave the same figure. Machine weight is 92 lb on Keiser's page and different elsewhere, so it is not on the card. These figures match our exercise bike guide.",
  },
  {
    id: "wahoo-kickr-bike-pro",
    name: "Wahoo KICKR BIKE PRO",
    brand: "Wahoo",
    category: "Smart bike with grade simulation and virtual shifting",
    badge: "Best for Cyclists",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wahoo+KICKR+BIKE+PRO&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$3,999.99",
    priceBounds: {"low":3999.99,"high":3999.99},
    specs: "20% incline and -15% decline, tilting frame · 2,500 W max · ±1% power accuracy · riders 5 ft 0 in. to 6 ft 4 in., 250 lb max · ANT+, ANT+ FE-C, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi · 1-yr warranty · pedals not included",
    pros: [
      "Physically tilts up 20% and down 15% to match the virtual road, according to Wahoo",
      "Virtual shifting that mimics Shimano, SRAM and Campagnolo shifters and gearing",
      "Five-point fit adjustment to copy your road bike, with five crank lengths",
      "Open connectivity: Zwift, the Peloton App or any trainer app",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The most expensive bike here, with a one-year warranty",
      "A 250 lb rider limit, the lowest on this page",
      "No screen and no pedals in the box",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For road cyclists who want a dedicated indoor bike that rides like their own. Everyone else gets more for less elsewhere.",
    description: "The KICKR BIKE PRO is Wahoo's top smart bike, and it is the alternative for people who find a Peloton too much like a spin class. Wahoo's page and a second US retailer, R&A Cycles, agree on the essentials: a frame that tilts to simulate grades from 20% uphill to 15% downhill, a 2,500 W maximum, power accuracy of plus or minus 1%, a rider height range of 5 ft 0 in. to 6 ft 4 in. and a 250 lb (113 kg) maximum rider weight. Wahoo adds virtual shifting that can replicate Shimano, SRAM and Campagnolo groupsets, a five-point fit system set up through its app, crank lengths from 165 to 175 mm, and ANT+, ANT+ FE-C, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi and direct connectivity. Pedals are not included, and the warranty is one year. On 19 October Wahoo listed it at $3,999.99 and an Amazon listing appeared in search results. The cheaper KICKR BIKE SHIFT ($1,999.99) was out of stock on Wahoo's store that day. We found no in-depth independent review to cross-check ride claims, so treat 'ride feel' as Wahoo's description.",
  },
  {
    id: "wahoo-kickr-core-2",
    name: "Wahoo KICKR CORE 2",
    brand: "Wahoo",
    category: "Direct-drive smart trainer for your own bike",
    badge: "Best If You Own a Road Bike",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wahoo+KICKR+CORE+2+Smart+Trainer&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$549.99",
    priceBounds: {"low":549.99,"high":549.99},
    specs: "1,800 W max · 16% max simulated grade · ±2% power accuracy · 130/135 mm QR, 12x142 and 12x148 thru-axle · 11-speed cassette or Zwift Cog pre-installed · ANT+ FE-C, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi",
    pros: [
      "Turns a road bike you already own into a smart bike for a fraction of the price",
      "Controls its own resistance in ERG and simulation modes, which a spin bike cannot do",
      "Ships with a cassette or Zwift Cog, so it is ready to ride, per Wahoo and OutdoorGearLab",
      "OutdoorGearLab's best value direct-drive trainer",
    ],
    cons: [
      "You need a compatible bike, and the bike has to be mounted each time unless it lives there",
      "No handle, and the 1,800 W and 16% limits are lower than flagship trainers",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For anyone with a road or gravel bike: the cheapest route to a smarter indoor ride than any Peloton.",
    description: "If you already own a road or gravel bike, a smart trainer is the most capable Peloton alternative per dollar: you remove the rear wheel, mount the bike, and the trainer sets its own resistance for structured workouts or virtual hills. Wahoo and OutdoorGearLab agree on a 1,800 W maximum, a 16% maximum simulated grade, axle fittings for 130 and 135 mm quick-release and 12x142 and 12x148 thru-axles, a pre-installed cassette or Zwift Cog, and ANT+ FE-C, Bluetooth and Wi-Fi. Wahoo quotes power accuracy of plus or minus 2%. It was $549.99 on Wahoo's and Zwift's stores and on Amazon on 19 October. The Peloton App will run on your tablet beside it, but Zwift and other trainer apps are where it shines. Our smart bike trainer guide covers this trainer and its rivals in detail, including the cassette and axle checks to make before buying.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Home Gym", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/home-gym/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Peloton Alternatives", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-peloton-alternatives/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best Peloton alternative?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most people, the Schwinn IC4 with Peloton App+. On 19 October 2026 the IC4 was $899 on Schwinn and App+ was $24 a month, about half the three-year cost of Peloton's cheapest bike with All-Access. If you want a large screen and automatic resistance, the NordicTrack X24 with iFIT is the closest Bike+ alternative.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use the Peloton App on a Schwinn IC4?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Schwinn lists the Peloton App among its compatible apps. Choose App+ at $24 a month, because App One limits you to three bike, tread or row classes a month. The app cannot read or set the IC4's resistance, so you follow the instructor's cues using the bike's own console.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much is Peloton App+ compared with All-Access?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On Peloton's site on 19 October 2026, App+ was $24 a month and the All-Access Membership was $44 a month; App One was $12.99. Over three years that is $864 for App+ against $1,584 for All-Access, a $720 difference. All-Access is required to use content on Peloton's own equipment.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is Echelon a good Peloton alternative?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It looks like one, but OutdoorGearLab reports that Echelon bikes now work only when connected to Echelon's servers, tying the bike to its membership. That is more lock-in than Peloton, not less. If you want an open bike, an IC4 or Keiser works with apps you choose and keeps working without a subscription.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Peloton bike recalled?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Two Peloton models have been recalled for seat posts that can break. The original Peloton Bike, model PL01, was recalled in May 2023, and the Original Series Bike+, model PL02 with serial numbers starting with T, in November 2025. Owners get a free replacement seat post. Peloton's current Cross Training Bike and Bike+ are not part of either recall.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a smart trainer better than a Peloton?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For cyclists who own a road bike, often yes: a trainer like the Wahoo KICKR CORE 2 ($549.99 on 19 October) sets its own resistance for workouts and virtual hills, which a Peloton bike cannot do in the same way, and you ride your own bike's fit. For non-cyclists who want classes, a Peloton or IC4 is simpler.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-19",
      dateModified: "2026-10-19",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 6,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-peloton-alternatives/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/best-peloton-alternatives/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "home-gym");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-19",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-19",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best peloton alternative",
      "peloton app on schwinn ic4",
      "peloton app+ vs all-access",
      "nordictrack x24",
      "peloton bike recall",
      "cheaper than peloton",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Best Overall: Schwinn IC4", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/home-gym/">Home Gym</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buying Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Best Peloton Alternative Bikes, Apps and Three-Year Costs</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        You can keep Peloton's classes and drop most of its price, or drop Peloton altogether for a bike that does something it cannot. Here are six currently sold bikes, what the Peloton App costs on each compared with an All-Access Membership, which closed platforms to be wary of, and the recalls to check before buying a used Peloton.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated October 19, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#nordictrack-x24"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/311CG4fIleL._SL500_.jpg" alt="NordicTrack X24 Bike" width="426" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#keiser-m3i"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31b09AaUZOL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Keiser M3i Indoor Bike" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>NordicTrack X24 Bike · Keiser M3i Indoor Bike</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Most people searching for a Peloton alternative want one of two things: Peloton's classes for
          less money, or a better bike than Peloton sells. The first is easy — Peloton's own app runs on
          other bikes, and its top app tier costs a little over half the membership a Peloton bike needs.
          The second depends on what "better" means to you: auto-adjusting resistance and incline, a
          studio-grade frame, or a smart bike that rides like your road bike. The six picks below cover
          both, and the guide after them does the membership arithmetic, explains what you lose when the
          app and the bike come from different companies, and covers the Peloton recalls that matter if
          you are tempted by a used one instead.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Overall:</span> <a href="#schwinn-ic4">Schwinn IC4</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Same Bike, Other Badge:</span> <a href="#bowflex-c6">BowFlex C6 Bike</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Bike+ Alternative:</span> <a href="#nordictrack-x24">NordicTrack X24 Bike</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Premium:</span> <a href="#keiser-m3i">Keiser M3i Indoor Bike</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best for Cyclists:</span> <a href="#wahoo-kickr-bike-pro">Wahoo KICKR BIKE PRO</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[4].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best If You Own a Road Bike:</span> <a href="#wahoo-kickr-core-2">Wahoo KICKR CORE 2</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[5].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these bikes,
          and no figure on this page is our own measurement. Each was confirmed on 19 October 2026 as
          currently sold on its maker's US store (Schwinn, BowFlex, NordicTrack and Wahoo) or, for the
          Keiser, through Amazon, and Amazon US search results that day showed listings for the Schwinn
          IC4, NordicTrack X24, Keiser M3i, Wahoo KICKR BIKE PRO and KICKR CORE 2; the BowFlex C6 rests on
          our earlier Amazon catalogue check. Figures were checked on that date against at least one
          independent source — Garage Gym Reviews, OutdoorGearLab and a second retailer — and the four
          bikes this page shares with our exercise bike guide carry the same figures. A figure appears
          in a product's specification line only where sources agree; where they conflicted we left it
          out and say so. Membership prices are those on Peloton's, iFIT's and the makers' sites on 19
          October 2026; prices and terms change often. We earn a commission if you buy through the
          Amazon links on this page; it does not change which products we list or what we say about
          them. <strong class="text-gray-900">What this page is, and is not.</strong> We review
          equipment; this is not medical or physiotherapy advice. If you have a heart, joint, back or
          balance condition, or are returning from injury, ask a doctor or physiotherapist what kind of
          exercise suits you before you buy.
